A provider yields a predeclared basic value (int); a consumer needs *int. The
value/pointer bridge covers basic types too, so plumb holds the int in a local
and passes its address (&n) rather than making *int a separate injector input.
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
output: plumb_gen.go
-- example.com/app/app.go --
package app

//plumb:build
func Count() int { return 3 }

//plumb:build
func Use(n *int) string { return "" }
